ll information  literacy which is not just you know  knowing like how to parse information  it's knowing where to go for quality  information so you know this is very  very basic stuff if you want to know in  YouTube for example what's available in  any given persons channel you can search  within that channel you can also you  know use various tags to search a  variety of different channels it's very  easy to do those who want to know more  about my my work can easily just type in  Gregory B Sadler into Google and all  sorts of sites come up including you  know my my facebook page my academia.edu  where you can see all of my academic  writings you know people ask do you have  a business well just again Google  Gregory B Sadler reason IO comes up  right do you do this sort of thing well  go to the site and search around within  the site same thing with the blog's do  you have a blog entry about this you  know you look on blogs and you see down  the side there's all these topics you  click on those or just use the search  function right this is this is very  important for people to learn and I  understand that you know there are some  users who because of the equipment that  they've got or the training that they've  had might not know how to do this but  it's really really to your advantage to  know how to carry out searches  effectively and efficiently because it  opens up an entire world for you and  knowing then how to aggregate stuff is  also really important as well so I think  that I do want to stress that if you are  like writing an author and asking do you  have any books about XYZ and you're not  just looking on say Amazon or Google to  find that out or if you're going to a  YouTube video producer and saying you  haven't  about XYZ and you're not looking say  through their playlists or even is using  the search function your shortchanging  yourself  you know and that doesn't mean that you  can't you know start a conversation in  so many other ways with somebody who who  you liked maybe you're posting it  because you hope you get a conversation  going you know it's better to be  well-informed and then start the  conversation so that brings me to my  second point about this which is that  you know if I had infinite time I might  actually be inclined to answer all of  those sorts of enquiries instead of just  saying listen you can search that do it  yourself right but I don't and and my  activity as a producer of content is  dependent on those same 24 hours that  everybody in the world gets and you know  I'm also in my middle age so I see that  horizon of what I'd like to get done  getting closer and closer and closer as  as I age as well so I don't really have  the time to walk people to be the sort  of information repository about what I  what I have available I've already done  the work to organize my videos into  playlists to you know do the tagging in  blogs for you know subject lines and all  of that to make my website manageable so  you know it's up to the the person on  the other end to do the work to walk  through it and I simply don't have the  available time to devote to each person  asking me questions about you know do  you have this do you have that do you of  this because that takes away from from  the thought and time and and work that I  do that got you thinking you should ask  me a question in the first place so  hopefully when you see this if you've  actually watched this to the end your  response is not that sob Sadler he  should have answered my question but  rather oh yeah I can answer my own  questions and that's empowering and  that's gonna help me out and then you  know you can come and ask me more  in-depth questions later on once you've  figured out what you  want to talk about her want to research  about  [Music]

